/-!
# Constructibility of the etale compact-support candidate

Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0GL0, theorem-constructible-shriek, and
section-derived-lower-shriek-compactification, finite-ring bounded-below specialization
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-etale-compactification-constructibility).
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Etale

/-- The candidate `Rp_* j_! K` for any fixed compactification has constructible cohomology
when the map is separated of finite presentation between qcqs schemes, the coefficient
ring is finite, and `K` is bounded below with constructible cohomology.
Source: Stacks Project, Tag 0GL0, theorem-constructible-shriek, and
section-derived-lower-shriek-compactification
(blueprint: lem-weil-pbc-etale-compactification-constructibility).

Proof plan (the seven steps of the node):

1. A finite ring $\Lambda$ is Noetherian: every ideal is generated by its finite set of elements.
The sequence $0,1,2\cdot1,\ldots$ repeats, so some positive $N$ kills $1_\Lambda$, and therefore
every $\Lambda$-module, including all sections and cohomology sheaves considered here, is killed
by $N$. Thus the torsion and Noetherian coefficient hypotheses of the cited Stacks inputs hold
without assuming invertibility. By [lem-weil-pbc-etale-shriek-compactification-independence], we
may choose a convenient compactification whenever needed and return to the originally fixed one at
the end. For every auxiliary separated finite-type map to a qcqs base used below, [thm-nagata-
compactification] supplies a compactification with quasi-compact open immersion and proper second
map; the later steps check these hypotheses before applying the compact-support inputs.

2. We record the passage from sheaves to bounded-below complexes for any fixed compactification
$(j,p)$ of a map $h$. By [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence], its proper ambient scheme is
qcqs. Extension by zero is exact [lem-weil-pbc-extension-by-zero-exact]; applying [lem-sp-exact-
functor-preserves-homology] to cycles and boundaries gives $\mathcal H^s(j_!L)=j_!\mathcal H^s(L)$
and preserves any lower bound $a$. The module sheaf categories are Grothendieck abelian [lem-weil-
pbc-module-sheaves-grothendieck-abelian], and $p_*$ is additive left exact by [def-weil-pbc-
direct-image]. Apply [thm-sp-truncation-e2] to $p_*$ and a bounded-below representative of $j_!L$:
its page is $E_2^{r,s}=R^rp_*j_!\mathcal H^s(L)=\mathcal H^r(Rh_!\mathcal H^s(L)[0])$, it vanishes
unless $r\geq0,s\geq a$, and its abutment is $\mathcal H^{r+s}(Rh_!L)$ with a finite filtration.
If $Rh_!$ sends every constructible sheaf to constructible cohomology, every $E_2$ is
constructible. By [thm-constructible-serre], each outgoing differential has constructible kernel.
The image of an incoming differential is the kernel of its cokernel and is therefore constructible
as well. Since the square of the differential is zero, this image lies in the outgoing kernel; the
cokernel of that inclusion is the next-page term $E_{r+1}^{r_0,s_0}$ and is constructible.
Induction on the page keeps every term constructible; each relevant entry stabilizes at a finite
page by the spectral-sequence theorem. For total degree $t$ only $a\leq s\leq t$ occurs. Induction
over its finite filtration, using extension closure, proves constructibility of $\mathcal
H^t(Rh_!L)$. The same theorem shows the output vanishes below $a$, so it remains bounded below.
This is a conditional argument, not an invocation of the item being proved.

3. For any affine base $B$, [lem-weil-pbc-etale-affine-line-shriek-sheaf-constructible] gives the
sheaf hypothesis of step 2 for $\mathbf A^1_B\to B$. All coefficient hypotheses were checked in
step 1. Step 2 therefore proves the assertion for every bounded-below constructible complex on
this affine line.

4. For affine $Y$, prove the assertion for $\mathbf A^n_Y\to Y$ by induction on $n$. For $n=0$ use
the identity compactification: identity extension by zero and identity direct image are the
identity, directly from [def-weil-pbc-projection-compactification]. For $n>0$, polynomial
coordinates in [def-nagata-relative-affine-space] identify the projection with $\mathbf
A^1_{\mathbf A^{n-1}_Y}\to\mathbf A^{n-1}_Y\to Y$. The intermediate scheme is affine. The first
factor sends a bounded-below constructible complex to one of the same kind by step 3, and the
second does so by induction. Apply [lem-weil-pbc-etale-shriek-qcqs-composition] to identify the
composite candidate with the candidate for $\mathbf A^n_Y\to Y$. The factors are separated of
finite presentation since polynomial rings have finite presentations and their affine diagonals
are closed.

5. Now suppose $X=\operatorname{Spec}B$ and $Y=\operatorname{Spec}A$. By [lem-weil-pbc-lfp-affine-
opens-finite-presentation], write $B=A[T_1,\ldots,T_n]/(u_1,\ldots,u_m)$. This quotient presents
$f$ as a closed immersion $i:X\hookrightarrow\mathbf A^n_Y$ followed by projection. The closed
immersion is of finite presentation because its ideal is generated by the displayed finite list,
and is quasi-finite because each fibre is either empty or a closed subscheme of a point. Apply
[lem-weil-pbc-etale-quasifinite-shriek-constructible] to obtain constructible cohomology for
$Ri_!K$. It is bounded below as follows: choose its identity open compactification, so it is
$Ri_*K$; the functor $i_*$ is left exact and its module categories are Grothendieck abelian, hence
[lem-weil-pbc-derived-functor-negative-vanishing] preserves the lower bound. Step 4 applies to
$Ri_!K$. Composition [lem-weil-pbc-etale-shriek-qcqs-composition] identifies this output with
$Rf_!K$.

6. Keep $Y$ affine and let $X$ be arbitrary as in the statement. Cover $X$ by finitely many affine
opens $U_1,\ldots,U_m$; this is possible because it is qc. Each restriction is locally of finite
presentation: at points of an open, shrink the affine presentation neighbourhoods to principal
opens contained in it, using $B_b=B[t]/(bt-1)$. Its source is qcqs, so [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-
morphism-permanence] makes it of finite presentation. Restrictions remain separated: their
diagonals are the base changes of the closed diagonal along the open subschemes of the relative
product; [lem-weil-pbc-proper-fp-permanence] preserves closed immersions under base change.
Constructibility of the restricted cohomology sheaves follows from [lem-weil-pbc-constructible-
qcqs-affine-local], since an affine open of a restriction is still an affine open of $X$;
restriction is exact [prop-weil-pbc-inverse-image-modules-exact] and commutes with cohomology by
[lem-sp-exact-functor-preserves-homology]. Induct on an upper bound $m$ for the number of affine
opens in such a cover, including empty opens. The case $m=1$ is step 5. For $m>1$ put $U=U_1$,
$V=U_2\cup\cdots\cup U_m$. By [lem-weil-pbc-separated-affine-open-intersection], $U\cap V$ is
covered by the $m-1$ affine opens $U_1\cap U_i$. The induction hypothesis applies to $V$ and
$U\cap V$, while step 5 applies to $U$. The triangle [lem-weil-pbc-etale-shriek-relative-mayer-
vietoris] and the long exact cohomology theorem [lem-sf-derived-homology-long-exact] give, in
every degree $t$, an exact sequence $A^t\to B^t\to\mathcal H^t(Rf_!K)\to A^{t+1}\to B^{t+1}$, with
$A=R(f|_{U\cap V})_!K|_{U\cap V}$ and $B=R(f|_U)_!K|_U\oplus R(f|_V)_!K|_V$. The end terms are
constructible; finite sums are constructible by extension closure. The middle term is an extension
of $\operatorname{coker}(A^t\to B^t)$ by $\ker(A^{t+1}\to B^{t+1})$, so [thm-constructible-serre]
proves it constructible. If $m=0$, $X$ is empty and the empty covering forces every sheaf and its
derived image to be zero.

7. Finally cover $Y$ by affine opens $W$. By [lem-weil-pbc-qcqs-morphism-permanence],
$X_W=X\times_YW$ is qcqs and $f_W$ is of finite presentation; it is separated because its diagonal
is the base change of the closed diagonal, using [lem-weil-pbc-proper-fp-permanence]. The
restriction of $K$ to the open $X_W$ has constructible cohomology: every affine open of $X_W$ is
an affine open of $X$, the affine-local criterion applies there, and exact inverse image commutes
with cohomology. Apply step 6 to $f_W$. Apply [thm-cbc-finite-torsion] to the cartesian square
with common scheme base $S=Y$, bottom map $W\to Y$ and top map $X_W\to X$. The map $f$ is
separated of finite type, $W\to Y$ is arbitrary as permitted by that theorem, and the coefficients
are finite. Thus its output equals $(Rf_!K)|_W$; [prop-weil-pbc-inverse-image-modules-exact] and
[lem-sp-exact-functor-preserves-homology] identify its degree-$t$ cohomology with $\mathcal
H^t(Rf_!K)|_W$. All these restrictions are constructible. The affine-local equivalence [lem-weil-
pbc-constructible-qcqs-affine-local] now gives constructibility on the original qcqs $Y$. Step 1
identifies this result with the originally fixed candidate $Rp_*j_!K$.
-/
theorem isConstructibleEtaleModuleSheaf_homology_compactSupportCandidate
    {X Y : Scheme.{u}} [CompactSpace X] [QuasiSeparatedSpace X]
    [CompactSpace Y] [QuasiSeparatedSpace Y]
    (Λ : Type (u + 1)) [CommRing Λ] [Finite Λ]
    (f : X ⟶ Y) [IsSeparated f] [LocallyOfFinitePresentation f]
    [QuasiCompact f] [QuasiSeparated f] (c : Compactification f)
    (K : _root_.DerivedCategory.Plus (Sheaf X.smallEtaleTopology (ModuleCat.{u + 1} Λ)))
    (hK : ∀ q : ℤ, Scheme.IsConstructibleEtaleModuleSheaf
      ((_root_.DerivedCategory.homologyFunctor _ q).obj K.obj)) (q : ℤ) :
    Scheme.IsConstructibleEtaleModuleSheaf
      ((_root_.DerivedCategory.homologyFunctor _ q).obj
        ((rightDerivedLowerShriekOfCompactification (ModuleCat.{u + 1} Λ) c).obj K.obj)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry.Etale
